0 @N1@ NOTE How a French claim came to rest in Spain (1713–1989)
1 CONT Told by Heirloom example
1 CONT
1 CONT Under the old French rule, the crown passed to the eldest son, then through male descendants only, and could not be given up.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1713 Philip V, Louis XIV's grandson and already King of Spain, renounced his French rights as the price of peace. When the senior French line ended with the Count of Chambord in 1883, the Legitimists argued that the renunciation had never been valid, so the claim belonged to Philip's senior male descendant.
1 CONT
1 CONT That was the Carlist branch until it died out in 1936. It then passed to Alfonso XIII, to his eldest son Jaime, Duke of Segovia, to Jaime's son Alfonso, and in 1989 to Louis Alphonse.
1 CONT
1 CONT Orléanists disagree. They say the renunciation stands, which makes the head of the Orléans branch the heir.
0 @N2@ NOTE February 1984 (1984)
1 CONT Told by Heirloom example
1 CONT
1 CONT In February 1984 Alfonso was driving his two sons when the car crashed. Francisco, eleven, died of his injuries; Louis Alphonse, nine, survived. Five years later Alfonso himself died in a skiing accident in Colorado, and the fourteen-year-old Louis Alphonse became head of the line.
0 TRLR
